WEBVTT

00:00:06.683 --> 00:00:10.046
A supply desk needs quantities for named items.

00:00:10.046 --> 00:00:13.552
A map links each unique key, an item's name, to a

00:00:13.552 --> 00:00:14.983
value, its quantity.

00:00:27.015 --> 00:00:30.372
String and Integer are the key and value types.

00:00:30.372 --> 00:00:34.158
Integer is the object form of int, required here as a

00:00:34.158 --> 00:00:35.158
type argument.

00:00:35.158 --> 00:00:39.015
Java converts each supplied number automatically; that

00:00:39.015 --> 00:00:41.015
conversion is called boxing.

00:00:41.319 --> 00:00:44.965
Put stores a value, replacing any value already under

00:00:44.965 --> 00:00:45.584
that key.

00:00:45.584 --> 00:00:48.542
Get retrieves by key, and size counts keys.

00:00:48.542 --> 00:00:51.569
Predict the three printed lines, then pause.

00:00:56.390 --> 00:00:59.487
The later put replaces two with three for map.

00:00:59.487 --> 00:01:00.833
Kit still holds one.

00:01:00.833 --> 00:01:03.189
There are two keys, so size is two.

00:01:03.189 --> 00:01:06.420
These print statements choose the order; HashMap

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:08.440
traversal does not promise it.

00:01:08.492 --> 00:01:11.905
Now change only the last put to store five under map.

00:01:11.905 --> 00:01:15.318
Which output line changes, and why does the number of

00:01:15.318 --> 00:01:16.542
keys stay the same?
